x=\"410.98857142857145\" cy=\"85.82608695652175\" r=\"6.5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"235.0\" y=\"422\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What the forecast promised</text><text class=\"chart-label\" x=\"15\" y=\"200.0\" font-size=\"12.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 15 200.0)\">How often it actually rained</text></svg>\n<figcaption>Green band = where a <b>flawless</b> forecast would still land, given\nhow few days we have. Red dots fall outside it, so those are real errors rather\nthan noise. The faint horizontal line is climatology\n(35%).</figcaption></figure>\n","season":"\n<h2 id=\"season\">Does the season matter?</h2>\n<p>In Melbourne the forecast is most skilful in\n<b>spring</b> (0.36) and least in\n<b>winter</b> (-0.05).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\">\n<table><thead><tr><th>Season</th><th class=\"num\">Days</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Rain days</th><th class=\"num\">Skill score</th>\n<th class=\"num\">Avg error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>winter</td><td class='num'>174</td><td class='num'>20%</td><td class='num'>-0.049</td><td class='num'>16.2%</td></tr><tr><td>autumn</td><td class='num'>180</td><td class='num'>47%</td><td class='num'>0.190</td><td class='num'>13.6%</td></tr><tr><td>summer</td><td class='num'>183</td><td class='num'>42%</td><td class='num'>0.340</td><td class='num'>12.9%</td></tr><tr><td>spring</td><td class='num'>217</td><td class='num'>32%</td><td class='num'>0.363</td><td class='num'>9.5%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n</div>\n<p class=\"muted\">Four-way splits of a two-year record are thin, so read the\nordering as indicative. Coarser binning is used here than for the annual curve\nfor the same reason.</p>\n"},"lat":-37.814,"lon":144.96332,"n":754,"n_cities":134,"name":"Melbourne","offset_days":-1,"pop_map":[{"hi":0.0719999999999999,"lo":0.0,"mean":0.00837748344370861,"n":302,"obs":0.08278145695364239,"sig":true},{"hi":0.22,"lo":0.0719999999999999,"mean":0.1271641791044776,"n":67,"obs":